Z||000000||filthyscarecrow||08-13-2000||09:09 PM||||i have a garage (a bit of a luxury for a college student i suppose) adn i hope to have some spare time this winter. i'm perfectly happy embarrassing 944's but i'd like to run with the vettes when i go on the track. methinks i need a turbo. i would like to do it myself because it *should* be cheaper and i am actually really good at stuff like that. my question is, where the HELL do you put the turbo? i've seen dennis lagrua's version, but mine is a manual, and i can't put it on the bellhousing as there are shift cables there. does anyone have the IRM or ace turbo kits for the manual gearbox? i would like to knwo where they put the turbo. also, i know the garrett/airesearch T3 works, but what about one off an eclipse gs-t? (i don't know what brand they are)iv'e heard those work too. right now i'm just doing a shitload of research to get an idea of what i'm up against. nay help/pics would be greatly appreciated. bye bye.<P>------------------<BR>me go fast.||||reg||1
Z||000001||SCCA FIERO||08-13-2000||11:09 PM||||On a manual Fiero, the turbo is usually put under the cruise control.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.spinfrenzy.com/turbo/images/PLUMBING.JPG"> <P>------------------<BR>-Chris <BR>88 Fiero Coupe<BR><B>KEEP THE EXCITEMENT ALIVE!!</B>||||reg||1
Z||000002||EdsB52||08-14-2000||02:43 AM||||What is the cost of a turbo kit for the 2.8? Who are good manufacturers, etc.? Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||08-14-2000||03:29 PM||||This kit is from Design One. This is the stage II kit that runs about $4,000 and the stage I kit goes for $3200 for a manual Fiero.  <A HREF="http://www.designonesystems.com" TARGET=_blank>www.designonesystems.com</A> <P>------------------<BR>-Chris <BR>88 Fiero Coupe<BR><B>KEEP THE EXCITEMENT ALIVE!!</B>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Greg Piet||08-14-2000||04:52 PM||||I just wanted to bring up another option which I would much rather do than a turbo.<P>RSM motorsports out of Canada <A HREF="http://webhome.idirect.com/~rsm1/" TARGET=_blank>http://webhome.idirect.com/~rsm1/</A> <BR>sells a supercharger kit for 3000 US.  This is a little cheaper than Designones turbo kit.<P>The flaw is that you must remove your AC compressor.  So if you can do w/o AC the supercharger may be a better route.  Just remember NO TURBO LAG!<P>Since my AC compressor died, I have been debating on whether to fix the AC or to put in one of these.  I am living in Las Vegas, which gets pretty hot in the summer, and AC would be nice, but I am currently surviving without the AC.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||08-15-2000||02:14 AM||||If you need any help installing one, just ask!!<P>Not that I've ever installed one before but I'd be more than willing to try to help.. heh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 149,000 miles and counting!     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 08-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||EdsB52||08-16-2000||07:19 PM||||Thanks to all who replied with info. I think the way to go would be the supercharger, however, I didn't like the dyno chart provided because it illustrated stock performance with HP and torque vs RPM, and with the supercharger it illustrated HP vs Speed. The comparisons didn't make sense.||||reg||1
